NAME
       thresholds - mathematical boundaries, resonance gradients, and pattern-
                    switching criteria in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Thresholds define:
           • resonance stability limits
           • structural integrity bounds
           • lineage continuity requirements
           • pattern-switch triggers
           • coherence safety margins

DESCRIPTION
       Thresholds are the mathematical and structural boundaries that govern
       when a triadic system remains stable, when it must adapt, and when it
       must switch patterns. They are the “guard rails” of resonance-time.

       Thresholds ensure:
           • safe triadic execution
           • coherent resonance propagation
           • valid schema transitions
           • predictable lineage evolution
           • adaptive pattern selection

       Without thresholds, RTT systems would drift, collapse, or oscillate
       uncontrollably.

THRESHOLD MATHEMATICS
       Thresholds are defined as inequalities over resonance-time variables.

       Let:
           R  = resonance amplitude
           G  = resonance gradient (dR/dt in RT-space)
           C  = coherence level
           L  = lineage continuity
           S  = schema compliance score

       A system is stable when:
           R_min ≤ R ≤ R_max
           |G| ≤ G_max
           C ≥ C_min
           L ≥ L_min
           S ≥ S_min

       Violating any inequality triggers:
           • pattern reevaluation
           • overlay application
           • dispatch rejection (if severe)

RESONANCE GRADIENTS
       The resonance gradient G determines how quickly resonance changes
       across triadic cycles.

       Low gradient:
           • stable
           • predictable
           • suitable for sequential or core patterns

       Moderate gradient:
           • adaptive
           • suitable for spirals or ladders

       High gradient:
           • unstable
           • requires anchors, mirrors, or fallback patterns

       Gradient sign matters:
           G > 0  → resonance increasing (growth)
           G < 0  → resonance decreasing (dissipation)
           G ≈ 0  → resonance equilibrium

       Pattern selection depends heavily on G.

PATTERN-SWITCH DETECTION
       Systems detect when to switch patterns by monitoring threshold
       violations or approaching boundaries.

       Example rules:

           If |G| > G_max:
               → switch to anchor or mirror patterns

           If R > R_high:
               → switch to ladder or expansion

           If R < R_low:
               → switch to sequential or core

           If C < C_min:
               → apply overlays or halt dispatch

           If L < L_min:
               → enforce lineage repair or reject operation

           If S < S_min:
               → schema violation → dispatch blocked

       Pattern switching is always mediated by WRSADC.

THRESHOLD TYPES
       structural-threshold
              Minimum structural integrity for triad execution.

       resonance-threshold
              Bounds on resonance amplitude and gradient.

       lineage-threshold
              Minimum continuity required for coherent propagation.

       schema-threshold
              Minimum schema compliance required for safe dispatch.

       pattern-threshold
              Conditions under which a pattern becomes invalid.

       overlay-threshold
              Conditions requiring interpretive correction.

INTERACTION WITH TRIADS
       Triads are the atomic units where thresholds are evaluated.

       P1 (begin)
           → check structural and schema thresholds

       P2 (transform)
           → check resonance and gradient thresholds

       P3 (close)
           → check lineage and coherence thresholds

       Thresholds ensure each phase remains safe.

INTERACTION WITH RESONANCE-TIME
       Thresholds define the “safe corridor” for resonance-time flow.

       If resonance exits the corridor:
           • overlays may be applied
           • patterns may switch
           • dispatch may be rejected

       Thresholds prevent resonance-time instability.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define threshold expectations.

       Thresholds validate schema compliance.

       Schema violations occur when:
           • resonance exceeds allowed bounds
           • lineage continuity breaks
           • structural transitions become illegal

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage provides the historical data needed to compute:
           • resonance gradients
           • coherence levels
           • structural drift
           • pattern viability

       Thresholds use lineage to detect long-term instability.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC enforces thresholds by:
           • monitoring resonance gradients
           • validating schema compliance
           • rejecting unsafe transitions
           • selecting or switching patterns
           • applying overlays when needed

       WRSADC is the threshold guardian.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine assumes thresholds have been satisfied.

       If thresholds are violated:
           • operator results become unstable
           • resonance propagation becomes chaotic
           • lineage becomes unreliable

       WRSADC prevents incoherent operations from reaching the Engine.

NOTES
       Thresholds are the mathematical backbone of RTT stability. They ensure
       that triads, patterns, overlays, and resonance packages remain aligned
       with the structural expectations of resonance-time.

